Wait — the candidates are `wide` and `compact` (distinct instances), each containing `shared`. Good. But note: `HStack.children(shared, Text...)` — `children` takes `View...`; using `shared` inside two distinct parents is allowed by our reject (only same instance as candidate). Good.

However, there's a subtlety: `SizeThatFits.make(Fit.of(wide, 640f), Fit.of(compact))` — `Fit.of` requires non-null. Good.

Test with an "identity-stable node" (fake pinned id): Since media isn't on main, I'll simulate by building a `View` whose `render` returns a `PathlandNode` and meanwhile record... but the pin can't survive assignIds. Hmm. The issue says "with and without a media controller". On main there's no media. I'll instead test the helper directly for the identity-stable duplicate (test 3). For test 1, I'll add a comment that identity-stable nodes are covered by the guard test. Alternatively, simulate a reused node by making a custom `View` that returns the *same* `PathlandNode` instance on every render. Then on mount, `assignIds` would assign the same id twice to the same node object (two positions in the tree). Wait — if two candidates both contain a custom view that returns the *same* `PathlandNode` instance, then the tree has the same node object in two places. `assignIds` would visit it twice and assign id 1 then id 2 to the same object; `duplicateIds` scanning the tree would see... the same object with a single `id` field = 2 in both positions. So duplicate id 2! That's actually a way to simulate the bug without media: a custom `View` that returns a cached/shared `PathlandNode`. And it's realistic: a developer's custom view that caches its node.

But wait, our reject only rejects the same `View` instance as *candidates*, not a shared `PathlandNode`. And in this scenario the candidates are distinct `View`s each embedding the same `PathlandNode`-returning view. That's a "shared subtree with a cached node" — a genuine bug. The guard would catch it at mount. This is a great test of the guard end-to-end: a `View` whose `render` returns a shared `PathlandNode` used in both candidates → mount throws. Let me add that as the "fails loudly" test, plus the direct `duplicateIds` helper test.

Actually, does `assignIds` on the same Node object twice produce a duplicate? Let's trace: root VStack → SizeThatFits slot → candidate A (HStack) → sharedNode; candidate B (HStack) → sharedNode (same object). `assignIds` pre-order: assigns root=1, slot=2, ... sharedNode in A = 5, ... then in B sharedNode = 9 (overwrites the field to 9!). Now the tree has the object referenced twice, but its `id` field is 9. When scanning, position A's child (the same object) has id 9, position B's child has id 9 → duplicate 9. Also id 5 is now unused. So `duplicateIds` returns [9]. The guard throws. 

But actually there's a subtle issue: the same object appearing twice means it has ONE id, so the tree would have two "positions" with id 9; the scan sees id 9 twice. Yes duplicate. Good test.

Let me write:
```java
@Test
void sharedRetainedNodeAcrossCandidatesFailsLoudly() {
    // A custom view that returns the SAME retained node every render (a cached
    // node) materializes one PathlandNode in two candidate positions. assignIds
    // writes the same id into both positions, so the frame would carry two nodes
    // with that id — the guard must fail loudly instead of corrupting id-keyed
    // consumers (the media-controller pinning is the real-world instance of this).
    PathlandNode shared = new PathlandNode(Components.TEXT);
    View cached = new View() {
